@page "/forms"
@inherits AppComponentBase

<PageOutlet Url="forms"
            Title="Forms and validation"
            Keywords="Blazor form validation, Blazor EditForm, Blazor DataAnnotations, Blazor validation message, Blazor server validation, ShowValidationMessage, BitFocusOnInvalidSubmit, Bit.BlazorUI forms"
            Description="How the bit BlazorUI inputs work inside a Blazor EditForm: the data annotations of the model, the message under each field, the focus on the first invalid field of a failed submit, the field CSS classes, and errors only the server knows about." />

<DocsArticle Eyebrow="Documentation"
             Title="Forms and validation"
             Lead="The bit BlazorUI inputs are Blazor form inputs. Inside an EditForm they are validated by the form, through the same EditContext the framework's own inputs use. A few opt-in parameters add what the framework leaves to the app: the message under each field, and the focus on the first invalid field when a submit fails."
             GitHubUrl="Forms/FormsPage.razor">

    <Badges>
        <BitTag Size="BitSize.Small" Variant="BitVariant.Outline" Color="BitColor.SecondaryForeground" Text="EditForm" />
        <BitTag Size="BitSize.Small" Variant="BitVariant.Outline" Color="BitColor.SecondaryForeground" Text="DataAnnotations" />
        <BitTag Size="BitSize.Small" Variant="BitVariant.Outline" Color="BitColor.SecondaryForeground" Text="Core and Extras" />
    </Badges>

    <Actions>
        <BitButton Size="BitSize.Large" Href="/forms#a-whole-form" IconName="@BitIconName.Play">
            See a whole form
        </BitButton>
        <BitButton Size="BitSize.Large" Variant="BitVariant.Text" Color="BitColor.SecondaryForeground" Href="/forms#which-inputs" IconName="@BitIconName.List">
            Which inputs
        </BitButton>
    </Actions>

    <ChildContent>
        <DocSection Id="at-a-glance" Title="At a glance"
                    Description="What an input does inside an EditForm, and the parameter or component behind each part.">
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <b>It joins the form.</b> <code>@@bind-Value</code> names the field, and inside an <code>EditForm</code>
                    the input reads the cascading <code>EditContext</code>, so the form's validator -
                    <code>DataAnnotationsValidator</code> or any other - validates it with the rest of the form.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <b>It shows the state.</b> A field that fails carries <code>aria-invalid="true"</code> and the invalid
                    look, and its root takes the framework's <code>valid</code>, <code>invalid</code> and
                    <code>modified</code> classes.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <b>It shows the message</b>, with <code>ShowValidationMessage</code>: the field's messages under it,
                    read out by a screen reader when the field takes the focus.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <b>It takes the focus</b>, with <code>&lt;BitFocusOnInvalidSubmit /&gt;</code> in the form: a failed
                    submit moves the focus to the first invalid field.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <b>It takes errors from the app</b>, with <code>ErrorMessage</code> and <code>Invalid</code>: a
                    rejection the app knows about, in a form or out of one.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                None of it replaces the validation of the framework: the attributes of the model are the rules, the
                <code>EditContext</code> holds the messages, and <code>ValidationMessage</code> and
                <code>ValidationSummary</code> keep working beside the inputs.
            </p>
        </DocSection>

        <DocSection Id="a-whole-form" Title="A whole form"
                    Description="Six kinds of input, one model of data annotations, a message under each field and the focus on the first invalid one.">
            <DemoExample Title="Sign-up form" RazorCode="@example1RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example1CsharpCode" CodeFiles="@example1CodeFiles" Id="example1">
                <div>
                    Submit the form empty: the focus lands on the name, which reads out its message. Fill a field in and
                    its message goes as soon as it passes; submit again and the focus moves on to the first field that is
                    still wrong.
                </div>
                <br />
                @if (signUpDone)
                {
                    <BitMessage Color="BitColor.Success">Account created for @signUpModel.Name.</BitMessage>
                    <br />
                    <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Outline" OnClick="ResetSignUp">Start over</BitButton>
                }
                else
                {
                    <EditForm Model="signUpModel" OnValidSubmit="() => signUpDone = true" novalidate>
                        <DataAnnotationsValidator />
                        <BitFocusOnInvalidSubmit />

                        <BitStack Gap="1rem">
                            <BitTextField Label="Name" Required ShowValidationMessage @bind-Value="signUpModel.Name" />
                            <BitTextField Label="Email" Type="BitInputType.Email" Required ShowValidationMessage @bind-Value="signUpModel.Email" />
                            <BitNumberField Label="Age" Required ShowValidationMessage @bind-Value="signUpModel.Age" />
                            <BitDropdown Label="Team" Placeholder="Pick a team" Required ShowValidationMessage Items="teamItems" @bind-Value="signUpModel.Team" />
                            <BitDatePicker Label="Start date" Required ShowValidationMessage @bind-Value="signUpModel.StartDate" />
                            <BitChoiceGroup Label="Plan" Horizontal Required ShowValidationMessage Items="planItems" @bind-Value="signUpModel.Plan" />
                            <BitCheckbox Label="I accept the terms" ShowValidationMessage @bind-Value="signUpModel.AcceptTerms" />
                            <BitButton ButtonType="BitButtonType.Submit">Create account</BitButton>
                        </BitStack>
                    </EditForm>
                }
            </DemoExample>

            <p>Four things make it:</p>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    The attributes of the model are the rules: <code>[Required]</code>, <code>[EmailAddress]</code>,
                    <code>[Range]</code>, <code>[AllowedValues]</code>. Their <code>ErrorMessage</code> is what the field
                    shows and what a screen reader reads out, so write it for a person.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <code>&lt;DataAnnotationsValidator /&gt;</code> runs the attributes, on every field as it is edited and
                    on the whole form when it is submitted.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <code>ShowValidationMessage</code> on each input shows its messages under it, and
                    <code>&lt;BitFocusOnInvalidSubmit /&gt;</code> takes the user to the first one.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <code>novalidate</code> on the <code>EditForm</code> leaves the checking to the form - the next section
                    says why.
                </li>
            </ol>
        </DocSection>

        <DocSection Id="novalidate" Title="The browser's own check"
                    Description="Why every form on this page carries novalidate.">
            <p>
                <code>Required</code> on an input marks it: an asterisk by its label, and the <code>required</code> or
                <code>aria-required</code> attribute, so a screen reader announces it as a required field. It validates
                nothing by itself - the <code>[Required]</code> of the model does. A browser, though, checks a
                <code>required</code> control of its own accord before it lets a form submit: it stops the submit, shows a
                bubble of its own in the language of the browser, and the <code>EditContext</code> is never asked. No message
                shows under the field, and none of the rules of the model runs until the browser is satisfied.
            </p>
            <p>
                <code>novalidate</code> turns that check off and keeps the attributes, so the form is validated once, by the
                rules of the model, with every message where the app put it. Keep <code>Required</code> on the inputs whose
                property is <code>[Required]</code>: it is how the user knows before submitting.
            </p>
            <CodeBox Language="cshtml">&lt;EditForm Model="model" OnValidSubmit="Save" novalidate&gt;</CodeBox>
        </DocSection>

        <DocSection Id="messages" Title="Showing the messages"
                    Description="ShowValidationMessage puts the messages of a field under it; the components of the framework still work beside it.">
            <p>
                With <code>ShowValidationMessage</code>, an input shows the messages the <code>EditContext</code> holds for
                its field under it, in the place its <code>ErrorMessage</code> goes, and references them through
                <code>aria-describedby</code>, so a screen reader reads them out whenever the field takes the focus. They are
                not announced as they appear: a submit validates every field at once, and a message from each of them read
                out in a row would bury the form under its own errors. The field that takes the focus says what is wrong
                with it.
            </p>
            <p>
                It is off by default, so a form that places its own <code>ValidationMessage</code> components does not show
                every message twice. To turn it on for a whole form, cascade it with <code>BitParams</code>. What a
                <code>BitParams</code> carries is a default, never an override: a field that sets
                <code>ShowValidationMessage</code> itself keeps its own value.
            </p>
            <CodeBox Language="cshtml">&lt;BitParams Parameters="formParams"&gt;
    &lt;EditForm Model="model" OnValidSubmit="Save" novalidate&gt;
        ...
    &lt;/EditForm&gt;
&lt;/BitParams&gt;

@@code {
    private readonly IBitComponentParams[] formParams =
    [
        new BitTextFieldParams { ShowValidationMessage = true },
        new BitNumberFieldParams { ShowValidationMessage = true },
        new BitDatePickerParams { ShowValidationMessage = true },
        new BitChoiceGroupParams { ShowValidationMessage = true },
        new BitCheckboxParams { ShowValidationMessage = true },
        new BitDropdownParams&lt;BitDropdownItem&lt;string&gt;, string&gt; { ShowValidationMessage = true },
    ];
}</CodeBox>
            <p>
                A <code>BitDropdownParams</code> reaches only the dropdowns of its own type arguments, so a form with
                dropdowns of other item or value types adds one for each pair.
            </p>
            <p>
                The messages belong to the <code>EditContext</code>, so the components of the framework show them too:
                <code>&lt;ValidationSummary /&gt;</code> lists them all above the form, and
                <code>&lt;ValidationMessage For="() =&gt; model.Email" /&gt;</code> shows one field's anywhere on the page.
                An <code>ErrorMessage</code> of the app's own wins the place under the field while it is set.
            </p>
        </DocSection>

        <DocSection Id="focus" Title="The focus on a failed submit"
                    Description="BitFocusOnInvalidSubmit takes the user to the first field to fix.">
            <p>
                A failed submit otherwise leaves the focus on the submit button, and a keyboard or screen reader user has to
                go looking for what to fix. Put <code>&lt;BitFocusOnInvalidSubmit /&gt;</code> anywhere inside the
                <code>EditForm</code>: it renders nothing visible, and once the validation of a submit is over it moves the
                focus to the first field of that form, in document order, that carries <code>aria-invalid="true"</code>.
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    Every input with a message slot carries it when its field fails, and so do the inputs of the framework. A
                    group hands the focus to the item the Tab key lands on: a ChoiceGroup to its checked radio, or its first
                    one.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Only a validation of the whole form moves the focus: a submit, or one the app asks the
                    <code>EditContext</code> for. A field validated as the user edits it never does.
                </li>
                <li>
                    A validator that finishes asynchronously (.NET 11) is waited for, and the focus is left alone if the user
                    starts editing a field before it finishes.
                </li>
                <li>It moves the focus in the browser, so it takes an interactive render mode.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                <BitLink Href="/components/focusoninvalidsubmit">BitFocusOnInvalidSubmit</BitLink> has the rest.
            </p>
        </DocSection>

        <DocSection Id="server-errors" Title="Errors only the server knows"
                    Description="A message added to the EditContext after the submit, shown and focused like the others.">
            <DemoExample Title="A taken user name" RazorCode="@example2RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example2CsharpCode" CodeFiles="@example2CodeFiles" Id="example2">
                <div>
                    The user names admin, root and bit are taken. Save one of them: the answer of the server shows under the
                    field, and the focus goes back to it. Change the name and the message goes.
                </div>
                <br />
                <EditForm EditContext="accountEditContext" OnValidSubmit="SaveAccount" novalidate>
                    <DataAnnotationsValidator />
                    <BitFocusOnInvalidSubmit />

                    <BitStack Gap="1rem">
                        <BitTextField @ref="userNameField"
                                      Label="User name"
                                      Required
                                      ShowValidationMessage
                                      @bind-Value="accountModel.UserName" />
                        <BitButton ButtonType="BitButtonType.Submit" IsLoading="accountSaving">Save</BitButton>
                    </BitStack>
                </EditForm>
                @if (savedUserName is not null)
                {
                    <br />
                    <BitMessage Color="BitColor.Success">Saved "@savedUserName".</BitMessage>
                }
            </DemoExample>

            <p>
                A data annotation can only check what the browser has: whether a user name is taken is for the server to
                say, after the submit. The way of the framework is a <code>ValidationMessageStore</code> of the app's own on
                the <code>EditContext</code> of the form. Add the message of the server to it and call
                <code>NotifyValidationStateChanged</code>, and the field shows it, carries <code>aria-invalid</code>, and is
                listed by a <code>ValidationSummary</code>, as it would for a message of the model.
            </p>
            <p>
                Two handlers keep the message true: it is about the value the server was sent, so editing the field
                (<code>OnFieldChanged</code>) or submitting again (<code>OnValidationRequested</code>) clears it.
            </p>
            <p>
                <code>BitFocusOnInvalidSubmit</code> does not move the focus here: the validation of the submit passed, and
                the message came after it. Call <code>FocusAsync</code> on the field yourself, after the notification, so
                the field already describes itself with the message when the focus arrives.
            </p>
            <p>
                For a rejection outside any form, or one the app keeps in its own state, set <code>ErrorMessage</code> on the
                input: it marks the field invalid and shows the text in the same place, and unlike a message of the
                <code>EditContext</code> it is announced by the live region of the field as it appears.
                <code>Invalid</code> marks the field without a text, for a reason explained elsewhere on the page. The
                <BitLink Href="/components/textfield">TextField</BitLink> page shows both.
            </p>
        </DocSection>

        <DocSection Id="styling" Title="Styling the state"
                    Description="The field classes of the framework, on the root of every input.">
            <p>
                Inside an <code>EditForm</code>, the root of each input carries the classes the <code>EditContext</code> gives
                its field, the same ones <code>InputText</code> carries: <code>valid</code> or <code>invalid</code>, with
                <code>modified</code> once the field has changed. They sit on the root, around the label and the messages,
                rather than on the native control. The inputs draw their own invalid look without them (the
                <code>bit-inv</code> class), so they are there for a stylesheet of the app - the one that already styles the
                inputs of the framework:
            </p>
            <CodeBox Language="css">/* the root of a field the user has changed, once it passes */
.modified.valid {
    border-inline-start: 3px solid var(--bit-clr-suc);
    padding-inline-start: 0.5rem;
}</CodeBox>
            <p>
                An app whose stylesheet expects other names, such as the <code>is-valid</code> and <code>is-invalid</code> of
                Bootstrap, sets a <code>FieldCssClassProvider</code> on the <code>EditContext</code>, and the inputs carry
                its names instead:
            </p>
            <CodeBox Language="csharp">editContext.SetFieldCssClassProvider(new BootstrapFieldCssClassProvider());

public class BootstrapFieldCssClassProvider : FieldCssClassProvider
{
    public override string GetFieldCssClass(EditContext editContext, in FieldIdentifier fieldIdentifier)
    {
        var isValid = editContext.IsValid(fieldIdentifier);

        if (editContext.IsModified(fieldIdentifier)) return isValid ? "is-valid" : "is-invalid";

        return isValid ? "" : "is-invalid";
    }
}</CodeBox>
            <p>
                An input with <code>NoValidate</code> carries none of them: it stays out of the <code>EditContext</code>
                altogether.
            </p>
        </DocSection>

        <DocSection Id="typed-values" Title="Values that do not convert"
                    Description="Text an input cannot turn into the bound type.">
            <p>
                Some inputs read typed text into the bound type: a number into a NumberField, a date into a DatePicker that
                takes typing, a color into a ColorPicker. Text they cannot read is not written to the model. The field gets
                a message in the <code>EditContext</code> instead, such as "The Age field is not valid.", so the form cannot
                be submitted with it. The message names the field by its <code>DisplayName</code>, or by the name of its
                property when it has none. The NumberField takes a <code>ParsingErrorMessage</code> of your own, and the
                date and time pickers an <code>InvalidErrorMessage</code>.
            </p>
        </DocSection>

        <DocSection Id="which-inputs" Title="Which inputs"
                    Description="Every input joins the EditContext; most also have a place for its messages.">
            <p>
                These have a message slot under the field, and with it <code>ErrorMessage</code>, <code>Invalid</code> and
                <code>ShowValidationMessage</code>:
                <BitLink Href="/components/checkbox">Checkbox</BitLink>,
                <BitLink Href="/components/choicegroup">ChoiceGroup</BitLink>,
                <BitLink Href="/components/colorpicker">ColorPicker</BitLink>,
                <BitLink Href="/components/datepicker">DatePicker</BitLink>,
                <BitLink Href="/components/daterangepicker">DateRangePicker</BitLink>,
                <BitLink Href="/components/dropdown">Dropdown</BitLink>,
                <BitLink Href="/components/fileinput">FileInput</BitLink>,
                <BitLink Href="/components/numberfield">NumberField</BitLink>,
                <BitLink Href="/components/rating">Rating</BitLink>,
                <BitLink Href="/components/searchbox">SearchBox</BitLink>,
                <BitLink Href="/components/slider">Slider</BitLink>,
                <BitLink Href="/components/textfield">TextField</BitLink>,
                <BitLink Href="/components/timepicker">TimePicker</BitLink>,
                <BitLink Href="/components/toggle">Toggle</BitLink>,
                and in Bit.BlazorUI.Extras
                <BitLink Href="/components/countrypicker">CountryPicker</BitLink>,
                <BitLink Href="/components/phoneinput">PhoneInput</BitLink>,
                <BitLink Href="/components/richtexteditor">RichTextEditor</BitLink> and
                <BitLink Href="/components/timezonepicker">TimeZonePicker</BitLink>.
            </p>
            <p>
                These join the <code>EditContext</code> the same way, but have no message slot of their own - put a
                <code>ValidationMessage</code> beside them:
                <BitLink Href="/components/calendar">Calendar</BitLink>,
                <BitLink Href="/components/circulartimepicker">CircularTimePicker</BitLink>,
                <BitLink Href="/components/fileupload">FileUpload</BitLink>,
                <BitLink Href="/components/otpinput">OtpInput</BitLink>,
                <BitLink Href="/components/tagsinput">TagsInput</BitLink>,
                and in Bit.BlazorUI.Extras <BitLink Href="/components/markdowneditor">MarkdownEditor</BitLink>.
            </p>
        </DocSection>
    </ChildContent>
</DocsArticle>
